{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1557969-a-succession-chronicle","id":1557969,"title":"A Succession Chronicle","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-01-28T15:33:56-08:00","id":1557969,"title":"A Succession Chronicle","content":"A while back, I had this idea to make a chronicle narratively inspired by the Succession HBO series. A story about a fight about who's going to control a given city after the timely death of a Prince/Archbishop is nothing new to Vampire, the Chicago metaplot and others are good examples of that, but I wanted to gravitate more to this idea of a family conflict, a sire dying and leaving his childer at each others throats. Although the concept is easy enough to execute in a Vampire game, some details ended up bothering me:<br /> <br /> 1) Should I pitch to the players a clan-only chronicle? Only focusing on a clan could really flesh out the politcs inside that clan, but at the same time I feel it could homogenize the character concepts. A clan-diverse approach could feed an interesting conflict of direct childer and adopted childer, but I don't know how much narratively coherent would be a sire that rules a city having a considerable amount of adopted childer.<br /> <br /> 2) Which Vampire game line to use? I think this fight of hereditary power I'm imagining is more appropriate to Vampire: The Dark Ages. The TV series is heavily inspired by Shakespeare's King Lear, so that should be a no-brainer, but the corporate trappings of the show are more akin to the modern politcking of the Camarilla. Also, your lineage seems more relevant to a Dark Ages setting than a modern one, considering that in the modern day there are more options to legitmize your temporal power beyond heirship.<br /> <br /> Beyond the questions above, I would like to hear your stories that had similar undertones of a fight for succession and how it went.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28986-agrat","name":"Agrat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28986_1.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-28T16:57:14-08:00","id":1557970,"title":"","content":"I would pitch to them as you describe it and ask THEM how they want to portray it - clan only, clan and generation with the same sire, political Camarilla domain with everyone being nominally tied to the Prince, or the Sabbat version, Dark Ages versus Modern, etc.<br /> <br /> It's a great concept, but the specifics are best left to your prospective players. not us. They know what would be fun and can hammer out the details with you. Whatever others tell you, it may or may not be the best fit for your gaming group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-28T17:20:15-08:00","id":1557971,"title":"","content":"If you're not interested in this answer to point 2, I'll drop it, but I would suggest Vampire: the Requiem even if you wanted to do something like use the Translation Guide to bring CofD stuff into WoD mechanics.<br /> <br /> My reasons for this are:<br /> <br /> 1) The Clan + Covenant setup avoids the homogeneity issue, having every PC be from the same Clan would leave Covenant open for greater PC diversity. Or they could be from different Clans for control in a city dominated by one Covenant.<br /> <br /> 2) Requiem's default assumption is that Elder vampires will go into voluntary torpor for long periods to weaken themselves as the curse makes it harder and harder for them to operate as their need for blood grows. Since there's no specific timer on this in-universe, it allows for tension about when a prominent Elder actually does decide to go nap for a century. It's planned, but said Elder can always delay a few months on a whim too. Masquerade has a more narrow set of narrative possibilities by having Final Death (or at least apparently) be the only real time such openings for power happen. <br /> <br /> 3) Requiem has a lot more focus on &quot;family&quot; bonds between sires and childer. For example an Elder could be a member of a Bloodline, and while any of their childer have the potential to join it as well, the assumed favored heir could already be a member, a different one could have joined a different Bloodlines of the Clan, or started their own, or a non-childe of the Elder but member of that Bloodlines could get in an edge from becoming blood-kin in a different fashion. This one isn't really necessary for the concept, but it can add depth and flavor that Masquerade's setup doesn't really have.<br /> <br /> 4) The lack of larger groups like the Camarilla or Sabbat, mean Requiem cities have an easier time being tuned to the themes of your chronicle focus. While not something in the same genre as you're asking about, in my first Requiem game, the Mekhet in my city were all blood relatives accept for the Mekhet PC who was brought in as a bit of a wildcard by the eldest Mekhet and actual ancestor of the others It's not something specific to Requiem by itself, but in combination with the points above it became a city-wide political move (and she basically managed to pull a Tom too), in a fashion that just hit harder than a VtM Elder with a quirk around Embracing mortal descendants breaking that streak would; it's just not a big deal in VtM outside of specific cases like the Giovanni.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-05T05:45:28-08:00","id":1558659,"title":"","content":"This sounds fantastic and I have not seen the series but as soon as I step away from my keyboard it will be added to my list. <br /> <br /> I will echo what others have said, have a session zero and dig into what they want to play. A game like these needs buy in from them as it no doubt will resort in them under minding each other, etc.<br /> Let them know what they are walking into sort to speak before hand. Hope it all goes well, and you keep us posted.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1680-justicar-seth","name":"Justicar-Seth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1680_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}